<p>David Berman, Fellow Emeritus of Trinity College Dublin, has died.<span id="more-57961"></span></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-57962 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/berman-david.png" alt="" width="516" height="340" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/berman-david.png 516w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/berman-david-300x198.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/berman-david-400x264.png 400w" sizes="(max-width: 516px) 100vw, 516px" /></p>
<p>Professor Berman was known for his work on the philosophy of George Berkeley, Irish philosophy, and philosophy of religion. He is the author of <em>A History of Atheism in Britain: From Hobbes to Russell</em> (1988), <em>George Berkeley: Idealism and the Man</em> (1994), <em>Berkeley </em>(1997), <em>Berkeley and Irish Philosophy </em>(2005), <em>Consciousness from Descartes to Ayer</em> (2021), and <em>Whately&#8217;s Historic Doubts about Napoleon&#8217;s Existence and the Preliminary Question </em>(2025), among other works. You can learn more about his writing <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/david-berman" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>Berman starting teaching at Trinity College Dublin in 1968 while earning his PhD in philosophy there. He earned an MA in philosophy from the University of Colorado at Denver, and his BA at the New School for Social Research. He was originally from Brooklyn.</p>
<p>He died on July 28th.</p>
